  Implementation had everything the blind variant lacked: exclusion search with the
  TT move removed from the node (sans-type compare, matching Move equality),
  verification at depth/2 around r_beta = tt_abs - 2*depth, extension only on fail-
  low, non-PV nodes only, start depth 8, LOWERBOUND-entry requirement, mate-band
  exclusion, per-line budget of 4 via a thread-level counter wrapped around the
  child call, TT cutoffs suppressed but ordering kept inside the subtree, store
  skipped for excluded nodes. Two tuning rounds pre-gate: v1 (TT-blind verification)
  inflated d10 startpos +59% nodes and BROKE the R3R1K1 depth-19 sweep - fifth
  variant killed by that test, this time by promiscuous extension in blown positions
  where every alternative fails low vs +30 pawns; the LOWERBOUND guard fixed the
  sweep (mate 5 at d19 again) and cut overhead to +7%. Unit gate 18/18 after fix.
  SPRT TC=2+0.02 Hash=8 a/b=0.1 elo1=20 CONC=1 vs fd8cef65: H0 accepted, Elo
  -6.5 +/- 23.6, LOS 29.6%. Verdict: at 7-11 ply there is no room - verifications
  cost real wall time and the population of depth>=8 LOWERBOUND nodes is tiny.
  Tree reverted bit-exact. The R3R1K1 sweep remains undefeated (5-0); keep the
  LOWERBOUND lesson attached to any future SE attempt.
